#{[17592186124870 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $6 \\times 6$ matrix and $\\text{Col}(A)={\\mathbb{R}}^{6}$?\n\n1. $A$ is not invertible\n2. $rank(A) = 6$\n3. $dim(Null(A)) = 6$"] [17592186134242 :question/problem "Solve for $\\mathbf{x}$ in $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ given that the matrix $A$ is orthogonal, and \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186272133 :question/problem "Solve for $\\mathbf{x}$ in $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ given that the matrix $A$ is orthogonal, and \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{15} & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} & \\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{15}\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186272133 :question/worked-solution "The inverse of an orthogonal matrix is equal to its transpose.\n\nHence, since $A$ is orthogonal, we have ${A}^{-1}={A}^{T}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\mathbf{x} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ {A}^{-1}A\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{T}\\mathbf{b} \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{15} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{15}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186275445 :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 8 & -11 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 8 & -11 & -2\\end{bmatrix}=3\\cdot 3\\cdot 4\\cdot (-2)=-72.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-72|=72.\n$$"] [17592186275446 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186275446 :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}=7\\cdot 3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 5=-105.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-105|=105.\n$$"] [17592186275447 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186275447 :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & 7 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & 7 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=(-3)\\cdot (-5)\\cdot 4\\cdot 3=180.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|180|=180.\n$$"] [17592186275448 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186275448 :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -4 & 12 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -4 & 12 & 5\\end{bmatrix}=6\\cdot 2\\cdot (-3)\\cdot 5=-180.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-180|=180.\n$$"] [17592186275449 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186275449 :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nNotice that the $2$nd row of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{3}} & -2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{-2}} & 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{4}} & 3 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $3$rd row of the $3\\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{-2}} & 1 & 5 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{1}} & 5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 3\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-6\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-6(4-25) \\\\  & =126.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|126|=126.\n$$"] [17592186275468 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186275468 :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nNotice that the $3$rd column of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 5 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 1 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{1}} & {\\color{lightgray}{4}} & 3 & {\\color{lightgray}{6}} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =3\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 4\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-120\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-120|=120.\n$$"] [17592186275469 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186275469 :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=3\\cdot 5\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 3=-45.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-45|=45.\n$$"] [17592186275470 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186275470 :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 7 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nNotice that the $1$st row of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 2 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ -2 & {\\color{lightgray}{7}} & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & {\\color{lightgray}{5}} & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & {\\color{lightgray}{5}} & 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $2$nd row of the $3\\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 10 & {\\color{lightgray}{3}} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 4 \\\\ -3 & 7 & {\\color{lightgray}{2}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 4\\cdot (-1)^{2+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 10 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 10 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8(-14+30) \\\\  & =128.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|128|=128.\n$$"] [17592186329656 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $4$th row contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $4$th row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(C) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{2}} & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{-4}} & 8 & -4 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{7}} & 7 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{4+1}\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 5 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 5 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 5 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & =-1\\cdot (5\\cdot (-4)\\cdot 3)=60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\ndet(C)=60.\n$$"] [17592186329698 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $4$th column contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $4$th column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(K) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 4 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ -2 & \\hspace{-0.167em}-1 & 0 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{8}} & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $3$rd column of the $3\\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(K) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{2}} & {\\color{lightgray}{1}} & 4 \\\\ -2 & -1 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ 0 & -1 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4(2-0) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338732 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $4$th column contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $4$th column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(G) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 4 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ 0 & 2 & 8 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{13}} & {\\color{lightgray}{6}} & {\\color{lightgray}{7}} & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & =(-2)\\cdot (1\\cdot 2\\cdot (-1))=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\ndet(G)=4.\n$$"] [17592186338734 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $3$rd row contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(H) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & {\\color{lightgray}{4}} & 0 \\\\ 7 & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}-3 & {\\color{lightgray}{8}} & 8 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 1 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ 10 & 0 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}-3 & 8 \\\\ 10 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}-3 & 8 \\\\ 10 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $1$st row of the $3\\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(H) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{7}} & -3 & 8 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{10}} & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(-6-0) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338735 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $2$nd column contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $2$nd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(P) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & -1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 8 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{1}} & -2 & {\\color{lightgray}{7}} & {\\color{lightgray}{1}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-1)^{4+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 8 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 8 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $3$rd column of the $3\\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P) & =-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{-1}} & 2 \\\\ -3 & 8 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (2\\cdot (-1)^{2+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{vmatrix}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4(8-(-12)) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 20 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338736 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $4$th column contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $4$th column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(Q) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{5}} & {\\color{lightgray}{2}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 5 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -4 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-1)^{1+4}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & =-5\\cdot (2\\cdot 1\\cdot 3)=-30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\ndet(Q)=-30.\n$$"] [17592186369845 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186369845 :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nNotice that the $4$th row of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $4$th row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & {\\color{lightgray}{5}} & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & {\\color{lightgray}{7}} & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & {\\color{lightgray}{-2}} & 2 & 1 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & -1 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (-1)^{4+2}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-12|=12.\n$$"] [17592186369852 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186369852 :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 0 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nNotice that the $2$nd column of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $2$nd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & -3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 5 & 0 \\\\ 10 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 7 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 2 & {\\color{lightgray}{1}} & {\\color{lightgray}{1}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{4+2}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $3$rd column of the $3\\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =2\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ 7 & 5 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{10}} & {\\color{lightgray}{7}} & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4(15+21) \\\\  & =144.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|144|=144.\n$$"] [17592186370953 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $3$rd column contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(F) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 0 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & -1 & {\\color{lightgray}{-2}} \\\\ 4 & 0 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} & =-1\\cdot (3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (-3))=-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\ndet(F)=-9.\n$$"]}
